{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED for exact head f9af5400df01607ad716286c12a593d7ae4547c5.\n\nFour blocking findings:\n1. The payload-tree inbox and trusted sequencer workflow are not isolated. If the inbox branch contains the workflow, the sender cannot trigger it; if a normal repository branch contains it, write-capable senders can modify push workflows and exfiltrate the sequencer credential. Put the control-plane workflow on an isolated trusted ref/repository and add a malicious-workflow threat spike.\n2. The proposed inbox ref name does not encode target plus namespace in its deduplication identity, and raw idempotency keys are not guaranteed valid Git ref components. Define a canonical, collision-resistant, ref-safe encoding over target, namespace, sender, and idempotency key.\n3. The TTL/non-atomic refusal protocol can silently lose an accepted request between payload publication, intake, refusal, and cleanup. Specify atomic ownership/acknowledgement and durable terminal records so accepted work cannot disappear without a visible outcome.\n4. The discoverability and durability claims overreach: ordinary clones do not fetch refs/seq, and an unwitnessed, unretained checkpoint cannot prove long-term history. Define explicit fetch/retention/replication policy and narrow the claims accordingly.\n\nVerified at this head: the concurrency-drain premise, corrected forge-availability boundary apart from the secret-isolation issue, R1 capability chain, R2-R7 and R10, make vet, make test, make build, and git diff --check.","body":{"head":"f9af5400df01607ad716286c12a593d7ae4547c5","tests":"make vet; make test; make build; git diff --check","verdict":"changes-requested"}}